QuickInstall - one server, one client
=====================================

The short path: a single machine that serves the image and carries the
session, and one thin client that boots off it. No domain, no load
balancer, no second server. HOWTO-lab-from-scratch builds the full thing;
this is what to do when you want to see a client boot today.

Debian 13 (trixie) on the server. Two network interfaces: one to the house
network, one for the clients. Everything below runs as root.


1. The archive
--------------

    install -d -m 755 /etc/apt/keyrings
    curl -fsSL -o /etc/apt/keyrings/ltsp6.asc \
        https://mabutz.github.io/ltsp6/ltsp6-archive-keyring.asc

    cat > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/ltsp6.sources <<'SRC'
    Types: deb
    URIs: https://mabutz.github.io/ltsp6
    Suites: trixie
    Components: main
    Architectures: amd64
    Signed-By: /etc/apt/keyrings/ltsp6.asc
    SRC

    apt-get update && apt-get install ltsp-server

Do not reach for [trusted=yes] if apt complains. It disables verification
for every package from the source, and the thing it is complaining about is
a missing keyring file.


2. The client-side interface
----------------------------

Give it a static address. The examples here use 192.168.67.1/24, which is
what the shipped dhcpd.conf and the iPXE script expect:

    ip addr add 192.168.67.1/24 dev eth1
    ip link set eth1 up

Make it permanent in /etc/network/interfaces or wherever this machine keeps
its addressing. A client cannot boot off an interface that comes up without
an address.


3. The image
------------

    ltsp-build-client \
        --base /srv/ltsp --arch amd64 --dist trixie \
        --components main,non-free-firmware \
        --apt-keys /etc/apt/keyrings/ltsp6.asc \
        --extra-mirror "https://mabutz.github.io/ltsp6 trixie main" \
        --early-packages ca-certificates,initramfs-tools \
        --late-packages console-setup,freerdp3-x11,kbd,ldm,linux-image-amd64,\
locales,ltsp-client-core,nbd-client,tftp-hpa,x11-xserver-utils,xinit,\
xserver-xorg-core,xserver-xorg-video-all \
        --purge-chroot --no-squashfs-image

Twenty minutes or so, most of it downloading. Three of those options repay
reading about in ltsp-build-client(8): --apt-keys and --extra-mirror are
what let the chroot install this project's own packages, and
initramfs-tools in --early-packages keeps dracut out of an image that needs
LTSP's initramfs hooks.

Then put it in place and tell nbd-server about it:

    ltsp-update-image --config-nbd -b /srv/ltsp amd64

No restart of nbd-server belongs here. It builds beside the live file and
renames, so a client already connected keeps reading the image it booted
with while the next one to connect gets the new one.


4. The services
---------------

    ltsp-config isc-dhcp-server     # or: ltsp-config dnsmasq
    ltsp-config nbd-server
    ltsp-config lts.conf            # a commented sample to start from

The first points the client at the boot file, the second writes the NBD
export. Both refuse to overwrite a file that is already there, which is
deliberate - pass --overwrite when you mean it. ltsp-config(8) lists what
else it can write: dnsmasq, kea, nfs.

Start them, and check that TFTP is being served out of the directory
ltsp-update-kernels wrote into:

    ltsp-info

That lists the chroots, the TFTP directories, the images, and both places a
client reads lts.conf from, saying which of the two wins.


5. The client's configuration
-----------------------------

The client reads lts.conf over TFTP from the directory its kernel came
from, so /var/lib/tftpboot/ltsp/amd64/lts.conf. The smallest useful one:

    [default]
        SERVER=192.168.67.1
        RDP_SERVER=192.168.67.1
        SCREEN_07=ldm
        LDM_SESSION=xfreerdp
        RDP_PREAUTH=ssh

RDP_PREAUTH=ssh is what makes a wrong password fail in the greeter, with a
message, instead of handing the client to the RDP server's own login
window. It needs the server's SSH host key to be known under the name in
RDP_SERVER - ltsp-update-sshkeys(8), and the SSH HOST KEYS section of
lts.conf(5).

Every other directive is in lts.conf(5). The ones most often wanted next:
LDM_THEME for the greeter's look, LANG for its language, SCREEN_02 for a
text console to debug from.


6. Boot the client
------------------

PXE, netboot enabled in its firmware, on the client-side segment. What you
should see: iPXE gets an address, fetches vmlinuz and initrd.img over TFTP,
the kernel mounts the NBD root, systemd starts, and ldm appears.

When it does not, PXE-network-boot-test walks the chain step by step and
says what each step looks like when it works. FAQ lists the symptoms that
have a known cause.
